WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:10.960
Welcome to our talk
on R Markdown, RStudio

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:11.827
Connect, and R Shiny.

00:00:11.827 --> 00:00:13.660
We're going to serve
you a three-course meal

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.810
today on how we automated our
data processing, error logging,

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.945
and alerts.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.190
So it's time to meet the chefs.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.760
My name is Kolbi
Parrish, and I'm

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.680
an informatics specialist
at the California

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.390
Department of Public Health.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.600
I'm working there through UCSF.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.055
Hi my name is Andy Pham.

00:00:34.055 --> 00:00:35.930
I'm also working at the
California Department

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.480
of Public Health, also for UCSF.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.100
So this talk was inspired
by a real-life event.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.420
It was early 2020,
and Andy and I

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.160
were working at the
California Department

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.560
of Public Health within a
small informatics section.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.350
And shortly after that,
the COVID-19 response

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.560
began, and our small section
was picked up and put

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.500
on to COVID-19 response,
where we remain today.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.620
One of our main
charges was to prop up

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.550
data pipelines and products
to help inform the response

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:12.920
efforts.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.260
And automation was a strategy
our team utilized early on

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.860
to help us be able to maintain
the scripts that we were

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.850
responsible for while
continuing to develop

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:29.150
based on the emerging needs.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.280
But as the number
of scripts that we

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.130
were responsible for
grew and grew and grew,

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.630
sometimes some months it felt
like they doubled or tripled.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.520
We quickly realized
that automation alone

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.290
wasn't enough to remain
responsive should anything

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:45.960
go wrong.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.130
So a group of us got together
and started tossing around

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:53.780
ideas on how we could implement
additional strategies to be

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.330
responsive quickly
should anything go wrong

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.970
with any of our scripts.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.610
And some of the strategies
that we came up with

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:07.260
were automated error-- data
processing, logging and alerts,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.600
as well as a process monitoring.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.600
So today that three-course meal
that we're going to serve you

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.480
starts with an appetizer of
how we automated our data

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.140
processing, followed by
a main course on error

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.400
alerts and logging.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.202
And for those
Shiny lovers, we're

00:02:23.202 --> 00:02:25.660
going to give you a taste of
Shiny in the form of a process

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:26.790
monitoring app at the end.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.720
So the way that we automated
our data processing

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.470
was through a combination of R
Markdown and RStudio Connect.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.930
We chose the import
from Git option that's

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.780
available on RStudio Connect.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.450
And I'll tell you a little bit
about why we chose that option,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.540
but it was really easy to use
because we store our code and Git

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.980
repositories.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.840
And essentially
what we needed to do

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.660
was store code where we
were already storing it

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.830
and make sure we had
a manifest JSON file.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.830
Grab that Git url, go
to RStudio Connect,

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.300
click on the content section,
click a publish button, and then

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.610
in a few steps we were able
to paste that URL that Git URL

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:15.870
into the screen and click a
couple additional buttons,

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:20.260
deploy our content, and we
had our automated scripts.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.020
But one of the things that we
really liked about this import

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.880
from Git option was
that it not only

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.920
helped us automate our
scripts, but it helped

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.750
us automate our pipelines.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.660
And that's because with
the check of a button,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.930
we were able to say, any time
we make a change to our Git

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.720
repository, have
that code change auto

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.720
flow to that published content.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.030
So great.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.550
Now we have
automation, but imagine

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.270
one of those many automated
scripts result in an error.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:55.360
How are you going to see what
error occurred and where?

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.900
Doing that can kind of be
like finding a single noodle

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.480
in a giant bowl of
messy spaghetti.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.730
Just a little bit frustrating.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:07.230
But error alerts and logging
are there to help.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.880
The way that we
implemented that was

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.370
through a combination
of R Markdown, blastula,

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.410
loggit, and RStudio Connect.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:18.910
So the process was really
similar for us to the way

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:20.950
that we automated
our data processing,

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.800
except we added in a
few additional layers

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.640
and those layers were logging,
email alert logic, and an error

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:28.860
report template.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.540
And for us that was in the
form of an R Markdown document

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:36.210
which added some structure
to those email alerts.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.640
So we started by calling
the loggit package,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.670
and we chose the loggit package
because it made it really easy

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.880
to create custom
error messages as well

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.950
as add in additional custom
fields to the log messages.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:53.830
And then we set up
our log path and this

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.050
helped specify where
we wanted the resulting

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.090
log files to be written to.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:00.740
And then we had
the strategically

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.680
placed the loggit function
calls throughout our script.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.100
And what we did is
we put a loggit call

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.830
at the start of each R
Markdown chunk upon error,

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.860
and we used the try catch
function to do this.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.210
I'll show you an example
in just a moment.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.270
And then at the end of
each R Markdown chunk.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:21.560
It was really important for us
to, within our R Markdown scripts,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:26.210
to name each chunk
with a unique name

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.965
because we then passed that
information back to the log.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.040
So this is an example of
a loggit call upon error

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:37.850
using the try catch function.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:42.430
And you see, in the event of
an error, we are calling loggit.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.960
It was pretty easy to use.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.570
This specific example is a
message, an error message type.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.490
So that's that first
parameter there.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:52.750
And then the next is
a custom error message

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.610
that we created use
using the paste function.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.910
So we essentially can
put any text that we want

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:02.660
and then pass that error
message to that paste function.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.800
And then the other
fields that were custom

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.550
that we found really helpful
to help us pinpoint exactly

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:13.270
where any issues were occurring
was the script name, the chunk

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:17.200
name, so the section of code
within a specific script.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:20.080
And then we also like
to log attempt numbers

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.480
because sometimes there are
chunks of code that will

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.620
retry in the event of an error.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.680
And then version
was another field

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:29.320
that we like to log because
it helps us determine

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.650
if the error occurred in a
production or a development

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:33.500
pipeline.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.900
So the resulting log file looks
a little something like this.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:43.810
You can see that we chose
to output a log file by day

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.390
and that was helpful because
we have a lot of scripts

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:48.460
and they're running
on a regular basis

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:51.560
and we have a lot
of log entries.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.900
So this helped us be able to
quickly just kind of organize

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:57.830
things by day.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.360
If you were to
open up that file,

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.420
it would look a little
something like this.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:04.550
It is a JSON-based format, so
it doesn need to be parsed.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:08.570
And Andy's going to cover how we
did that later on in the talk.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:11.360
But you'll notice that
the fields that are logged

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.360
are similar to what you saw
in that loggit function call.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:18.110
So that first one there
is an error message

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:21.350
and it's a log
level type of error.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:24.570
You could also log things
like informational messages,

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.110
or warnings as well.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.205
And then the next field there
is a custom error message.

00:07:30.205 --> 00:07:32.330
And if we were to look at
the rest of the log file,

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.670
we'd find those
additional custom fields

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:37.705
like script name,
chunk name, and version.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.120
So now that we have
our logs set up

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.638
and our logging
is happening, I'm

00:07:44.638 --> 00:07:46.180
going to cover a
little bit about how

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.950
we implemented error alerts.

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:52.930
And it was relatively
easy to do by just adding

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:56.560
a little extra code at the end
of each R Markdown chunk

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:58.960
outside of the try
catch function.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.830
And in the event of an
error, we essentially

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.830
use blastula to send out an email.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:08.950
And so that
highlighted code there

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:11.770
shows the section of
code that will actually

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:14.380
render the email using
a specified error report

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:15.230
template.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:17.200
That's the error
underscore report dot RMD,

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.690
and I'll show you what that
looks like in the next slide.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.500
And once that's rendered, then
blastula then blasts it out

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:28.060
to the viewers and
collaborators that you specify

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:32.789
when you set up the automation.

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:33.340
All right.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:38.280
So this is a screenshot of the
R Markdown error report template

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:39.460
that we use.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:41.789
We like to keep it really
simple and just include

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:45.300
the information that we find
helpful to pinpoint exactly

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:46.950
where any issues occurred.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.990
So that includes version, and
again that, for us, that

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:52.050
means development versus
production, the script

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:53.830
name, the chunk name.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:56.670
So exactly where that error
occurred within a script,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.060
the error itself.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:01.050
And then we also like to
include a path to the full log

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.030
file just in case we want
to look at the history there.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:07.340
So putting this all together,
how this works is we

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:10.310
have our script published
on RStudio Connect

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:11.930
and it's on the
schedule and it's

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:14.120
running on a regular basis.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.500
We have our logging
integrated into the script.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.340
We have that error alert
logic integrated, In the event

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.980
and an error, Connect will
essentially blast use blastula

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:27.470
and send out an email
to the specified viewers

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.030
and collaborators.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:32.330
That error email
follows the template

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:35.060
that I showed you earlier
and looks a little something

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:36.000
like this.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.930
This is just a dummy error
email that we set up.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:42.900
But you see here the
version is our dev pipeline.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.020
You see the script name, the
chunk name, the error itself.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:50.362
And then we also have a
spot for the log path there.

00:09:50.362 --> 00:09:51.820
So I'm going to
turn the floor over

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:55.510
to Andy to cover how we
took those log files that

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:58.030
were in JSON based format
and turn them into something

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.166
a little more digestible.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:02.150
All right.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:03.050
Thank you, Kolbi.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:06.010
So now you have your
automated processes set up

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:08.420
and you have your email
alerts for error logging.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.870
Now, let's say we get an email
saying that there's an error.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:14.230
The next step is to
troubleshoot, right?

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:17.290
However, errors rarely
occur in isolation

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.300
in order to fix an
error efficiently

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.270
and to prevent it from occurring
again in future states.

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:24.522
We want to be able to find
if this error is occurring

00:10:24.522 --> 00:10:26.480
in your development and
production environment.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.760
Has this error occurred multiple
times throughout your processes?

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.460
And so before the process
monitoring app, what we would

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.730
have to do is we had to go
through the error and process

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:40.850
logs and as Kolbi mentioned before,
these are in JSON format.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.000
And so and there's
probably hundreds

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.520
to thousands of lines
of this, so you'd

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.560
be pretty much
abusing your Control

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:51.610
F to try to find what errors
occurred and where it occurred.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:55.377
And that's also not to mention
that, since we're logging almost

00:10:55.377 --> 00:10:57.460
every day, we'd have to
go through all these files

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:00.520
to see if there's some kind
of historic pattern coming on

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.530
from these errors.

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:05.080
And so going through all
this, I would equate it

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.080
to trying to eat a
melting ice cream sundae.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.390
Everything is mixed together.

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:12.260
Different ice cream
flavors are mixing.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:15.880
And you might you're
tasting like two or three

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:16.630
different flavors.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:18.213
You're not sure if
you like it or not.

00:11:18.213 --> 00:11:19.660
It's very interesting.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:24.670
However, our approach was
to season in some R magic

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:28.180
and make a more beautiful
ice cream sundae where

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:29.890
all the components
are easily seeable,

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:31.265
and you can get
to where you need

00:11:31.265 --> 00:11:34.600
to be quickly and efficiently.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.300
And so we did this with this
was our process for our process

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:38.930
monitoring app.

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:43.220
We took a bunch of messy
logs, applied some loggit,  R

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:46.340
Shiny, plotly, data tables,
any R visualization

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:49.280
packages, and turned
it into a dashboard

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.310
where we can view both how
our processes were doing

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:57.290
and the errors we got
within one single dashboard.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:01.280
However, we first needed to be
able to read all of these logs

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.680
into something that
was R readable.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.710
Fortunately for us, loggit
provides this function already.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.340
There is a function called
read underscore logs where,

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:13.070
if you pass the path
of your error file,

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:16.910
it will read that error file
and return an R data table

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:17.970
that's easily filtered.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.150
So now we could filter
based on if we wanted

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:23.570
to find an error and then by
which specific process and then

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:25.890
within a specific time frame.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.950
So this was very
useful for putting up

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:30.350
the dashboard initially.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.230
And so we fed this data
into a data table call

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:36.680
and built error data tables where
we could view all of our error

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:40.340
data's errors in relation to
each other, when they occurred,

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.250
and if they occurred
within any other processes.

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:47.150
We additionally fed our process
logs into another data table,

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:50.330
and because we had set up our
loggit calls within each R Markdown

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:53.750
chunk, we can now see how
long each R Markdown chunk took

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:56.300
within the process and
be able to identify

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:00.570
any bottlenecks as needed.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:03.480
What we also did was we
also fed some of the process

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.137
log data into plotly.

00:13:06.137 --> 00:13:08.220
And so we created this
graph where we could easily

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:11.130
be able to see how our
processes were doing in relation

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:14.550
to each other and in relation to
our development and production

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:15.570
environments.

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.030
We could also see if any
processes were running much,

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:20.910
much longer than we expected
so that we can get in there

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.165
and fix it before
anything broke.

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:28.900
And so we took all of these
different pieces, the error data

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:30.850
tables, the graphs,
and we put it

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:34.100
all into-- used R Shiny  to
put it all into a dashboard.

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:36.100
And so now we could
get what we needed

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:38.710
to be really quickly
and be able to see

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:40.930
on both of our development
and production errors

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:44.050
and processes in one setting.

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.450
And so the outcome
of this is that we

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:49.780
had all of our error viewing,
troubleshooting and process

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:52.210
monitoring in one
place, and we could

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.430
get to where we
needed to be quickly.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:57.370
In addition, because
of R Shiny's sort

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:00.580
of user friendly
interface, we could

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:03.340
have new users on our
team be able to dive

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:06.400
into being able to fix
our errors without having

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.832
to know the intricacies
of our training system,

00:14:08.832 --> 00:14:10.540
because we already
had an interface built

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.100
over that layer.

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:15.010
And so a new hires to
our team could also

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:17.500
start contributing to this
process monitoring app

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:20.020
again without having
to know too much

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:22.900
or spend too much time learning
about how our error logging

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.450
system works.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:26.920
And so errors happen.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:28.540
We're all human.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:30.430
These are, we hope
these tools that we

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:33.700
and recipe that we have
made available to you today

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:36.850
help you be able to create
a full three-course error

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:39.970
friendly meal that's easily
digestible and worthy

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:41.800
of praise.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:43.650
Thank you.

